{#
    The head, once, for both layouts.

    The boot line is the load-bearing part, and it is shipped rather than
    written here: `soul-boot.js` is part of the system, because every surface
    with a mode switch needs the same four lines and a theme is not the place
    to keep a copy of them. `sds-theme` reads the mode from
    `document.documentElement.dataset.theme` when it upgrades — "what the boot
    line wrote" — and a site that never writes it hands the element nothing to
    show, so the switch renders with neither side pressed and the reader's
    choice is forgotten on the next page. A manual is many pages; that is not
    a corner case, it is every click.

    It runs before the stylesheets on purpose. A choice applied after first
    paint is a flash of the other mode, which is worse than no switch at all.

    It is not a module, and that is the point: `type=module` is deferred
    whether you ask for it or not, so a stored choice applied from `soul.js`
    would arrive as a flash of the other mode.

    The key is `soul-theme` by default. Two products on one origin are two
    keys — name another with `data-key` on the tag, and give `sds-theme` the
    same one.
#}
<meta charset="utf-8" />
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
{#- The project's name once. On a landing page the two are the same, and
    "Soul Design System — Soul Design System" is a tab nobody wrote. -#}
<title>{{ title }}
    {%- if env.projectNode.title and title != env.projectNode.title %} — {{ env.projectNode.title }}{% endif -%}</title>
<script src="{{ asset('/styles/soul-boot.js') }}"></script>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('/styles/soul.css') }}" />
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('/styles/document.css') }}" />
<script type="module" src="{{ asset('/styles/soul.js') }}" defer></script>
